%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 146b4dedcb9af3b4ab5fe5794271c3d8, type: 3}
  m_Name: Ticket_Coc1_Dust_Overheat
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::TicketData
  ticketId: ticket_coc1_dust_overheat
  targetModule: 0
  sceneName: COC1-Copy
  title: Overheats and shuts itself down
  description: It still works, but the fan is really loud from the moment I turn
    it on, and after about twenty minutes it just switches itself off. It sat in
    the stockroom for about a year before we started using it again.
  icon: {fileID: 0}
  briefingDialog: {fileID: 0}
  requiredTaskId: ticket_coc1_dust_overheat
  requiresDiagnosis: 1
  symptomText: Powers on and reaches Windows. Fans at full speed from cold. Thermal
    shutdown under sustained load.
  diagnosisOptions:
  - label: Cooling blocked by dust build-up
    isCorrect: 1
    feedback: "Correct. A year in a stockroom, and a fan running flat out from cold,
      both point the same way \u2014 the heat has nowhere to go."
  - label: Thermal paste has dried out
    isCorrect: 0
    feedback: A fair guess, and worth checking second. But dried paste alone rarely
      takes a machine to full fan speed from cold. Look at what is physically blocking
      the airflow first.
  - label: Failing power supply
    isCorrect: 0
    feedback: A failing PSU usually cuts out without warning, and it would not make
      the processor fan work harder. The fan speed is the clue here.
  - label: Faulty memory
    isCorrect: 0
    feedback: Bad memory gives you crashes and blue screens, not a machine that runs
      hot from the second it is switched on.
